Z||000003||WGraffiKs||01-11-2000||07:08 PM||||BM,<BR>What plugs RU using with all that "extra" spark voltage?  I'm wondering if the standard plugs will tolerate it without eroding the elecrtodes.  Perhaps a switch to the platinums would be in order if you upgrade the stock coil/wires?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-11-2000||08:11 PM||||WG,<P>Actually the extra voltage doesn't work the way you think. Normal plugs will handle it just fine in all but top end race motors. Even the many use normal plugs. The extra voltage capacity is there for when compression and other conditions need it, but thats not the normal firing voltage. Firing voltage is a function of plug gap, F/A ratio, and compression. It's not really controlled by the coil, but if a weak coil can't make the required power then bad spark.<P>The plugs in 'Makers motor are actually firing at about the same voltage as they should have before. Likely the old coil was going bad, may also be a flaky tack filter as well.<P>Bmaker, replace the tach filter. it's likely on its way south if the coil change helped that much. The filter can stall you at some annoying times if it fails. Since the tach is still jumpy I'd say it not breathing real well.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||01-11-2000||08:50 PM||||I don't know if it's the extra heat or what, but it sure seems like those 2.8 Fiero engines go through plug wires mighty fast. The 2.5 DIS (87-88) engines kind of do too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WGraffiKs||01-11-2000||09:20 PM||||So Ogre, sounds like what you're saying is that a switch to MSD coil Plug Wires won't give me a substantial performance increase over the stock setup in a "pretty-much" stock motor?||||reg||1
